WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.710
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.519
Have a look at these beautiful images.

00:00:07.519 --> 00:00:12.959
Representing these materials that you see
here takes more than 25 gigabytes of storage.

00:00:12.959 --> 00:00:17.970
You could store several feature-length movies
on your hard drive using the same amount of

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:18.970
storage.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:25.060
And this technique is able to compress all
this 25 gigabytes into 45 megabytes without

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.290
introducing any significant perceptible difference!

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:33.800
That is close to a whopping 500 times more
efficient representation.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:40.080
This improved representation not only helps
easing the storage requirements of these assets,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.480
but it is also makes the rendering times,
or in other words, the process of creating

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:50.550
these images via light simulation programs
typically more than twice as fast to process.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.390
That is a ton of money and time saved for
the artists.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:58.860
An important keyword in this piece of work
is anisotropic scattering.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.300
So what does that mean exactly?

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:05.079
The scattering part means that we have to
imagine these materials not as a surface,

00:01:05.079 --> 00:01:10.159
but as a volume in which rays of light bounce
around and get absorbed.

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:15.479
If we render a piece cloth made of velvet,
twill, or a similar material, there are lots

00:01:15.479 --> 00:01:20.770
of microscopic differences in the surface,
so much so, that it is insufficient to treat

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:24.319
them as a solid surface, such as wood or metals.

00:01:24.319 --> 00:01:26.700
We have to think about them as volumes.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.140
This is the scattering part.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.760
The anisotropy means that light can scatter
unevenly in this medium, these rays don't

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.219
bounce around in all directions with equal
probability.

00:01:37.219 --> 00:01:42.030
This means that there is significant forward
and backward scattering in these media, making

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:47.149
it even more difficult to create more optimized
algorithms that simplify these scattering

00:01:47.149 --> 00:01:48.319
equations.

00:01:48.319 --> 00:01:53.630
If you look below here, you'll see these colorful
images that researchers like to call difference

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:54.630
images.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:59.569
It basically means that we create one image
with the perfectly accurate technique as a

00:01:59.569 --> 00:02:00.569
reference.

00:02:00.569 --> 00:02:05.409
As expected, this reference image probably
takes forever to compute, but is important

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:07.119
to have as a yardstick.

00:02:07.119 --> 00:02:13.200
Then, we compute one image with the proposed
technique that is usually significantly faster.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.590
So we have these two images, and sometimes,
the differences are so difficult to see, we

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.410
no way of knowing where the inaccuracies are.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:26.450
So what we do is subtract the two images from
each other, and assign a color coding for

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:27.510
the differences.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:33.180
As the error may be spatially varying, this
is super useful because we can recognize exactly

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.079
where the information is lost.

00:02:35.079 --> 00:02:39.810
The angrier the colors are, the higher the
error is in a given region.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:44.300
As you can see, the proposed technique is
significantly more accurate in representing

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:48.620
this medium than a naive method using the
same amount of storage.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.610
This paper is extraordinarily well written.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:56.270
It is one of the finest pieces of craftsmanship
I've come along in long while.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:59.329
And yes, it is a crime not having a look at
it.

